{:edb/db-before-t 1340 :edb/db-after-t 1341 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534653 50 #inst "2026-10-12T06:04:14.262Z" 13194139534653 17592186045417 true] [17592186133711 1071 "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in $\\mathbb{R}^2,$ which of the following could be the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)?$\n\n- $\\left\\langle -y, \\: y\\right\\rangle$\n\n- $\\left\\langle -2x, \\: 2x\\right\\rangle$\n\n- $\\left\\langle 5y\\sin x, \\: x-5\\cos x \\right\\rangle$" 13194139534653 17592186045417 false] [17592186133711 1071 "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in $\\mathbb{R}^2,$ which of the following could be the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)?$\n\nI. $\\left\\langle -y, \\: y\\right\\rangle$\n\nII. $\\left\\langle -2x, \\: 2x\\right\\rangle$\n\nIII. $\\left\\langle 5y\\sin x, \\: x-5\\cos x \\right\\rangle$" 13194139534653 17592186045417 true] [17592186133712 1071 "Let $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\langle 2\\sin t, \\: 2 \\cos t \\rangle$ for $t \\in \\left [0, 2\\pi \\right).$ \nThe area of $D$ can be written as\n\n![](images/45/45513e4aedd37b868d2020cbbb47f49938f6ca03487ded8b5e519a2ec8e4edf5.svg)\n\nwhere $P(x,y) = -y$ and $Q(x,y) = 0.$ Determine the missing part of the equation.\n\n*Hint: Note that $C$ is negatively oriented.*\n\n![](images/a5/a5eeb5db90f9ee0d86a49e5bbf02629c7886fa019db7cff3c4d0e1eb43e8fd75.png)" 13194139534653 17592186045417 true] [17592186133712 1071 "Let $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\langle 2\\sin t, \\: 2 \\cos t \\rangle$ for $t \\in \\left [0, 2\\pi \\right).$ \nThe area of $D$ can be written as\n\n![](images/b6/b6cfc404b1866e7791c2a2e120133d5bb4c3bf586e9b3aadfda62042e70478b9.png)\n\nwhere $P(x,y) = -y$ and $Q(x,y) = 0.$ Determine the missing part of the equation.\n\n*Hint: Note that $C$ is negatively oriented.*\n\n![](images/a5/a5eeb5db90f9ee0d86a49e5bbf02629c7886fa019db7cff3c4d0e1eb43e8fd75.png)" 13194139534653 17592186045417 false] [17592186133895 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431048 13194139534653 17592186045417 true] [17592186133895 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431050 13194139534653 17592186045417 true] [17592186133896 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431049 13194139534653 17592186045417 true] [17592186133896 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431053 13194139534653 17592186045417 true] [17592186133897 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431051 13194139534653 17592186045417 true] [17592186133897 1038 #edb/ref 17592186431052 13194139534653 17592186045417 true] [17592186134040 1050 840.0 13194139534653 17592186045417 true] [17592186135131 1064 "Suppose that $\\mathbf F =P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2,$ and a region $D$ is bounded by a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve $C,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/fa/fa3efbc9200a6acdda01e503999f7588cd44fd63af77fa0a6abf1b5303c9a889.png)\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nLet's now consider what happens when the scalar curl term on the left-hand side of the above equation equals unity everywhere on $D.$ In other words, let's consider what happens when $$\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=1.$$\n\nIn this case, the double integral gives the area of $D,$ because\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=\\text{Area}(D).\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThus, we've just discovered a new method of computing the area of $D.$ We simply choose a vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)$ whose scalar curl equals unity everywhere on $D,$ and then compute its circulation over $C.$\n\nThere are infinitely many vector fields that we can use to compute the area of a region. Some of the most common for this purpose are as follows:\n\n$$\\mathbf{F}(x,y)= \\left\\langle -y, \\: 0 \\right\\rangle, \\qquad \\mathbf{G}(x,y)= \\left\\langle -\\dfrac{y}{2}, \\: \\dfrac{x}{2} \\right\\rangle, \\qquad \\mathbf{H}(x,y)= \\left\\langle 0, \\: x \\right\\rangle$$" 13194139534653 17592186045417 false] [17592186135131 1064 "Suppose that $\\mathbf F =P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2,$ and a region $D$ is bounded by a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve $C,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/fa/fa3efbc9200a6acdda01e503999f7588cd44fd63af77fa0a6abf1b5303c9a889.png)\n\nGreen's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's now consider what happens when the scalar curl term on the left-hand side of the above equation equals unity everywhere on $D.$ In other words, let's consider what happens when\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=1.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the double integral gives the area of $D,$ because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y=\\text{Area}(D).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThus, we've just discovered a new method of computing the area of $D.$ We simply choose a vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y)$ whose scalar curl equals unity everywhere on $D,$ and then compute its circulation over $C.$\n\nThere are infinitely many vector fields that we can use to compute the area of a region. Some of the most common for this purpose are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y)= \\left\\langle -y, \\: 0 \\right\\rangle, \n\\qquad\n\\mathbf{G}(x,y)= \\left\\langle -\\dfrac{y}{2}, \\: \\dfrac{x}{2} \\right\\rangle,\n\\qquad\n\\mathbf{H}(x,y)= \\left\\langle 0, \\: x \\right\\rangle\n$$" 13194139534653 17592186045417 true] [17592186135132 1064 "As a concrete example, let $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C,$ parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\langle 2\\cos t, \\: \\sin t \\rangle, \\qquad t \\in \\left [0, 2\\pi \\right).\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and its positively oriented bounding curve $C$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/a6/a6d9fe9cad4ccee56d5d61457c475b41371e0705f1aeccd3a18fb4a3fa8e127c.png)\n\nTo calculate the area of $D$ using Green's theorem, we need to find a vector field whose scalar curl equals unity everywhere on $D.$ So, let's choose\n\n$$\nP(x,y) = -y, \\qquad Q(x,y) = 0.\n$$\n\nIndeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 0 - \\left(-1\\right) = 1. \\qquad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $C$ is a positively oriented path, the area of $D$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting our expressions for $P$ and $Q$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C P \\: \\text{d}x + Q \\: \\text{d}y = -\\oint\\limits_C y \\: \\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's now evaluate the integral using the usual methods. The derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r'}(t) = \\langle -2\\sin t, \\: \\cos t \\rangle.\n$$\n\nSo we can calculate our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }2{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }2(\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{2})\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,dt \\\\  & ={[t-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =2\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(D)  =2\\pi.\n$$" 13194139534653 17592186045417 true] [17592186135132 1064 "As a concrete example, let $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C,$ parametrized as\n\n$$\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\langle 2\\cos t, \\: \\sin t \\rangle, \\qquad t \\in \\left [0, 2\\pi \\right).$$\n\nThe region $D$ and its positively oriented bounding curve $C$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/a6/a6d9fe9cad4ccee56d5d61457c475b41371e0705f1aeccd3a18fb4a3fa8e127c.png)\n\nTo calculate the area of $D$ using Green's theorem, we need to find a vector field whose scalar curl equals unity everywhere on $D.$ So, let's choose\n\n$$P(x,y) = -y, \\qquad Q(x,y) = 0.$$\n\nIndeed, we have $$\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 0 - \\left(-1\\right) = 1. \\qquad{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$$\n\nTherefore, since $C$ is a positively oriented path, the area of $D$ is given by\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\ & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\ & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSubstituting our expressions for $P$ and $Q$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\\oint\\limits_C P \\: \\text{d}x + Q \\: \\text{d}y = -\\oint\\limits_C y \\: \\text{d}x.$$\n\nLet's now evaluate the integral using the usual methods. The derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is\n\n$$\\mathbf{r'}(t) = \\langle -2\\sin t, \\: \\cos t \\rangle.$$\n\nSo we can calculate our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}-{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}t\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}t)\\,dt \\\\ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }2{sin}^{2}t\\,dt \\\\ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }2(\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}2t}{2})\\,dt \\\\ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }1-\\operatorname{cos}2t\\,dt \\\\ & ={[t-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}2t]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\ & =2\\pi \\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\\textrm{Area}(D) =2\\pi.$$" 13194139534653 17592186045417 false] [17592186135137 1064 "We've established that if a region $D$ is bounded by a positively oriented curve $C,$ and we select a vector field $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ such that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} =  1,\n$$\n\nthen the area of $D$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(D) =  \\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = \\oint\\limits_C P \\: \\text{d}x + Q \\: \\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nNow, if $C$ is *negatively* oriented, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(D) =  -\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = -\\oint\\limits_C P \\: \\text{d}x + Q \\: \\text{d}y.\n$$" 13194139534653 17592186045417 true] [17592186135137 1064 "We've established that if a region $D$ is bounded by a positively oriented curve $C,$ and we select a vector field $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j$ such that\n\n$$\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 1,$$\n\nthen the area of $D$ is given by\n\n$$\\textrm{Area}(D) = \\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = \\oint\\limits_C P \\: \\text{d}x + Q \\: \\text{d}y.$$\n\nNow, if $C$ is *negatively* oriented, then\n\n$$\\textrm{Area}(D) = -\\oint\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = -\\oint\\limits_C P \\: \\text{d}x + Q \\: \\text{d}y.$$" 13194139534653 17592186045417 false] [17592186431012 1080 "5{cos}^{6}⁡t{sin}^{4}⁡t" 13194139534653 17592186045417 true] [17592186431012 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534653 17592186045417 true] [17592186431012 1083 #uuid "031791c1-0f2f-546c-82e8-999c8554dc97" 13194139534653 17592186045417 true] [17592186431013 1080 "II only" 13194139534653 17592186045417 true] [17592186431013 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139534653 17592186045417 true] [17592186431013 1083 #uuid "0de6da70-c426-566d-b3d0-dce7471f5600" 13194139534653 17592186045417 true] [17592186431014 1080 "I and II only" 13194139534653 17592186045417 true] [17592186431014 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13